 so the LLM advantage is a real model effect rather than a capacity artifact; it strengthens their choice of fine-tuned LLMs (GPT-3.5-Turbo) for generating phytochemically enriched substitutions, and they can skip a wider GISMo.\"\n\nFor programme 1:\n- if_lever_changes: \"It would overturn the project's fixed-width default: the archived 300-dim GIN encoder would be shown to leave ranking quality on the table, and any future GISMo-style substitution ranker (including the personalized inverse-cooking integration) should scale the ingredient encoder width; width becomes a published scaling curve rather than an untested hyperparameter.\"\n- if_not_changes: \"It would confirm GISMo's 300-dim ingredient encoder was already at the plateau, validating the original fixed-width configuration and telling future work to seek gains from graph structure, context, or data rather than capacity.\"\n\nalready_done for programme 1: status \"partly\".\nclaim: \"Meta only ever trained/evaluated GISMo at a single ingredient-encoder width (hidden=300, emb_d=300); the paper's 5-run average is at that one width and no 64–512 sweep exists.\"\nurl: https://github.com/facebookresearch/gismo/blob/main/gismo/README.md\nquote: \"To train the best performing model, use this command in the gismo folder: python train.py name=GIN_MLP setup=context-full max_context=43 lr=0.00005 w_decay=0.0001 hidden=300 emb_d=300 dropout=0.25 nr=400 nlayers=2 lambda_=0.0 i=1 init_emb=random with_titles=False with_set=True filter=False\"\n\nalready_done for programme 2: status \"no\".\nclaim: \"Rita et al. reimplemented GISMo at a single 300-dim width (5 runs) and swept model families (DaVinci/TinyLlama/GPT-3.5), but never swept GISMo encoder width across 64–512.\"\nurl: https://www.mdpi.com/2504-4990/6/4/131\nquote: \"used an embedding dimension of 300 to represent ingredients in a continuous vector space. The model consists of two graph convolutional layers, each with 300 hidden units\"\n\ngaps:\n1. \"No programme links this home-recipe ranking metric to industrial formulation or environmental footprint: the card itself notes it 'shows ranking accuracy on home recipes, not industrial formulations, and never measures actual environmental footprint,' so no industrial ingredient-replacement-for-sustainability programme (e.g., plant-based formulation companies) would change practice purely on a Hit@1 number from Recipe1MSubs.\"\n2. \"The Meta GISMo repository is archived and read-only (Nov 2024), so the primary pipeline the card targets is no longer actively maintained; the result would feed the research literature more than a live product.\"\n3. \"Unresolved discrepancy: Rita et al.'s GISMo reimplementation reports Hit@1 34.53 ± 0.10% on the original Recipe1MSubs, while Meta's published GISMo test Hit@1 is 20.56 ± 0.14% — the card's 'reproduce 20.56 within 2 MRR points' check will have to explain this gap before any width-scaling conclusion transfers between programmes.\"\n\nno_programme_reason: \"\" (empty since I have programmes).\n\nWait, the schema: no_programme_reason should be a string; since I found programmes, I'll set it to \"\".\n\nLet me now write notes.md and then output JSON.\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether I should be even more precise on the \"step\" description for the card. The card's \"Compute axis: Substitution-ranker capacity (ingredient-encoder embedding width): 64–512 dimension, 4 levels.\" So the step = ingredient-encoder embedding width. For GISMo, this maps to `emb_d` (embedding dimension of the ingredient encoder) and `hidden` (GIN hidden dim). Both are 300. Good.\n\nFor the Rita programme, the step is the \"GISMo benchmark\" step — they reimplemented GISMo at 300 dim to serve as the baseline. So the card targets their baseline ranker's encoder width.\n\nLet me write everything now.\n\nOne more consideration: the task says \"Pick one to three.\" I'll pick two. That's within range.\n\nLet me write the notes.md file with my working notes, then fina